window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-26T05:10:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"Printed: Tamura speech (Kofu, 10:00): rate to neutral \"soon\", pace may accelerate; press conference 14:00 (not reported at this refresh). Pending: leading and coincident index revisions (Sep, 14:00; consensus 116.4 and 119.5); MoF primary-dealer meeting on 2027 issuance; US markets closed (Thanksgiving)","why":"Tamura set the hawkish marker; the dealer meeting sets the super-long issuance tone after the 40-year cleared at 4.09% on 2.63 cover."},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"Tokyo CPI (Nov, 08:30; consensus core 2.9% from 2.6%, headline 3.1% from 2.8%, core-core 2.7% from 2.6%); liquidity supply auction (5 to 11 years, ¥500bn); MoF investor meeting; US early close (13:00 ET)","why":"The early read for the 18 Dec national CPI; a core print at or above 2.9% reprices December after the slip to 76.7% and the 2-year close under 1.75%."},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"2-year JGB auction (¥2.8tn); Ueda speech and press conference (Nagoya); industrial production prelim (Oct); retail sales (Oct); unemployment and jobs ratio (Oct); housing starts (Oct); MoF intervention total (Nov)","why":"Ueda's last scheduled words before the December meeting; the October hard data test the household leg; the intervention total shows whether the MoF acted near 160."},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Manufacturing PMI final (Nov; flash 53.7); Q3 MoF corporate survey (capex); 10-year JGB auction; Huang testifies to the House","why":"Confirms the manufacturing leg of the regime and the capex input to the GDP second estimate; the Nvidia governance strand drives the Nikkei leaders."},{"date":"2026-12-02","event":"Consumer confidence (Nov; October 35.0)","why":"A print under 35 is the Slowdown trigger on the household leg."},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Services PMI final (Nov; flash 50.5); 30-year JGB auction; Huang testifies to the Senate","why":"A services final under 50 moves the regime to Slowdown; the 30-year auction follows a 40-year that cleared above consensus on 2.63 cover."},{"date":"2026-12-08","event":"Q3 GDP second estimate (08:50; first −0.1% annualized); wages and real wages (Oct); household spending (Oct); US FOMC starts (8–9 Dec, 97.8% for 25 bp)","why":"A second negative quarter on the revision is the Slowdown trigger; real wages are the virtuous-cycle test."},{"date":"2026-12-12","event":"Diet session ends; food consumption-tax cut bill due to pass the Upper House by this date","why":"About ¥5tn a year unfunded; the fiscal input to the super-long end."},{"date":"2026-12-15","event":"BoJ Tankan (Dec; September large manufacturers +24); machinery orders (Oct)","why":"The quarterly leading read two days before the BoJ meeting."},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"BoJ decision (meeting 17–18 Dec; pricing 76.7% for 1.50% per MogWatch); national CPI (Nov, 08:30) the same morning","why":"The base case is a hike to 1.50%; the CPI prints hours before the statement."},{"date":"2027-01-05","event":"USTR Section 301 comments and hearing requests due (hearing from 25 Jan 2027; Japan 15% in total, alternative 20%)","why":"The window in which the 20% alternative tied to the investment commitments is argued; final action expected early 2027."}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1 with Takata dissenting for 1.50%. October core CPI 2.2%, core-core 2.2%, headline 2.3%: at target; October services PPI +4.2% y/y, the fastest since March 1990. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov, 10:00): the Bank must bring the policy rate to a neutral level soon because financial conditions are still accommodative, must respond swiftly to signs of an upward deviation in prices \"including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\", and what is required is \"not to go slowly, but to hurry while unhurried\" (悠々として急ぐ); upside risks from Middle East raw-material costs, import prices on the weak yen and AI demand, with underlying inflation less firmly anchored than in the US or Europe; Fisco reports he said his view of neutral at about 2% is unchanged, Reuters that he did not restate the figure; the 14:00 press conference was not reported at this refresh. Pricing before the speech: MogWatch OIS 76.7% for 18 Dec (implied 1.442%, +19.2 bp; March 1.603%; 1.75% or higher by March 45%) at 01:01 JST on 26 Nov, from 78.2% on 25 Nov and 84.7% on 20 Nov; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. JGBs closed 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB): 2-year 1.720% (−5.5 bp, the first close under 1.75% since 10 Nov), 5-year 2.030% (−8 bp), 10-year 2.645% (−7.5 bp, lowest in about three weeks; Fisco 2.627%, stock-marketdata.com 2.620%), 20-year 3.580% (−4.5 bp), 30-year 3.950% (+1.5 bp), 40-year 4.180% (+6 bp), December futures 128.24 (+64 sen, high 128.39, the highest since 6 Nov): a bull-flattening to the 20-year with the super-long end selling off after the ¥300bn 40-year reopening cleared at 4.09%, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10 and was called somewhat weak by QUICK. Under the framework the 2-year under 1.75% says the market is pricing part of a hold, and Tamura the same morning says the Bank is not; the stance is Tightening with the pace in dispute between the market and the hawks. The yen: USD/JPY closed 159.17 in New York on 25 Nov (−0.09; high 159.41, low 158.66 when US stocks were down 3% to 4%), 158.95 to 159.24 in Tokyo on 26 Nov with 159.17 at noon and no visible move on Tamura; no MoF or Katayama comment dated 25 or 26 Nov was found. The Fed: October core PCE 3.3% y/y (consensus 3.2%), Q3 GDP revised to 4.3% annualized (3.5%), claims 189,000; CME 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (CappNotes; Gaitame 96.4%); US 10-year 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp), 2-year 4.61%. The balance sheet was ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct. Next: Tokyo CPI and the MoF investor meeting 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%), 2-year auction and Ueda in Nagoya 30 Nov, GDP second estimate 8 Dec, FOMC 8–9 Dec, national CPI and the BoJ decision 18 Dec."},"cbName":"BoJ stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The leading data still say Expansion: the November flash manufacturing PMI rose to 53.7 with export orders at their second-fastest growth since January 2018, October exports were a record ¥11.77tn (+20.5%), core machinery orders +13.4% y/y and the October services PPI printed +4.2% y/y, a 36-year high.","b":"The household leg is turning: services PMI 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low, the composite at a six-month low, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, consumer confidence 35.0, and the equity drawdown is 21.7% on the 25 Nov close.","read":"Leading over coincident keeps the regime Expansion on the manufacturing leg; the services side has not crossed 50 and the hard October data are not in yet, so the conflict is open.","trigger":"Services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec), consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves the regime to Slowdown."},{"a":"Breadth says the fall is over: on 25 Nov 993 Prime names rose against 537 (63.9%) and 23 of 33 sectors gained with pharmaceuticals +5.48% while the Nikkei fell 2.78%, TOPIX lost only 0.50%, and on 26 Nov 863 rose against 629 at 13:00 with the Nikkei up 1.2% to 1.5%.","b":"The index says it is not: Advantest and Tokyo Electron took about 1,328 points off the Nikkei on 25 Nov and SoftBank Group closed at a year-to-date low (−14.8%), then the same names added 700 to 875 on 26 Nov; the close of 57,011.62 is 5.0% under the 60,030 first-test line and the Nikkei VI spiked to 43.23 intraday before closing 33.39.","read":"The drawdown is the AI complex on both legs and the broad market is being bought, which is the best breadth read since the fall began; the Nikkei 225 is the index the posture is set on and it moves 1,600 points a day on four names.","trigger":"A close above 60,030 with TOPIX above 3,895 and the Nikkei VI under 35 moves the posture to Neutral; a close under 56,436.65 is a new drawdown low."},{"a":"The Bank says the pace may need to speed up: Tamura on 26 Nov wants the rate at neutral \"soon\", financial conditions are still accommodative, and the response to upside price signs should include \"accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\"; the services PPI at +4.2% y/y and core CPI at 2.2% give the case, and the Reuters poll has 86% of economists for December.","b":"The market is pricing part of a hold: the 2-year closed 1.720% on 25 Nov, the first close under 1.75% since 10 Nov, MogWatch OIS has December at 76.7% (84.7% on 20 Nov, 88% before the CPI miss), and the 40-year auction cleared above consensus on 2.63 cover.","read":"The drift is the equity drawdown and the haven bid, not the inflation data; Tamura is the hawk and Ueda has not spoken since 19 Nov, so the market is waiting for the governor, not the board.","trigger":"A 2-year close back above 1.85% after the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) reprices December; a market-stability reference from Ueda on 30 Nov is the hold tell; a 2-year close under 1.70% says the hold is the base case."},{"a":"The yen is off the haven path: USD/JPY closed 159.17 on 25 Nov with the Nikkei down 2.78%, was 159.17 at noon on 26 Nov with Tamura hawkish, and the front-end gap is about 289 bp with the Fed at 97.8% for 25 bp after core PCE 3.3%.","b":"The haven bid exists on broad US falls: the 158.66 low on 25 Nov came with the S&P 500 down 3.2% and the Nasdaq 4.6% intraday, and the pair gave it all back as they recovered; 160 is 83 sen away, no MoF or Katayama comment has been found since 19 Nov, and the November intervention total prints 30 Nov.","read":"An equity fall confined to AI names does not produce a yen bid and neither does a hawkish board member; the pair is trading the rate gap and the 160 line is the next event.","trigger":"A close above 160 brings the MoF; a return of the broad equity fall (TOPIX under 3,674) or a US liquidation that holds takes the pair toward 158.4 and the 155 to 156 carry-unwind zone."},{"a":"The belly says haven: the 10-year closed 2.645% on 25 Nov, the lowest in about three weeks, the 5-year fell 8 bp and futures hit 128.39, the highest since 6 Nov.","b":"The super-long end says supply: the 30-year rose to 3.950% and the 40-year to 4.180% the same day after the ¥300bn reopening cleared at 4.09%, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10, with the MoF dealer and investor meetings on 26 and 27 Nov and the unfunded food tax cut (about ¥5tn a year) in the Diet.","read":"The curve is bull-flattening to 20 years and steepening beyond it: the haven bid stops where the fiscal premium starts, and with the yen at 159 the warn pair stays on.","trigger":"A 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal and 4.30% the fiscal line; a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the warn pair off; a 40-year above 4.25% says the dealer meetings did not reassure."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The most hawkish board member says the pace may need to speed up on the morning the 2-year sits under 1.75% and December pricing is 76.7%; the market and the hawks disagree on the pace, not the direction. USD/JPY did not move (158.95 to 159.24).","src":"Reuters via Investing.com and AOL; Kyodo via OANDA Japan kn_2026112601000557; Fisco via Zaikei 879251; Investing.com jp; Yomiuri and Tokyo Shimbun via Beloud","tag":"Policy","title":"Tamura (Kofu, Yamanashi, 10:00): the BoJ \"needs to bring its policy rate to a level deemed neutral to the economy soon\" because financial conditions remain accommodative; given that underlying inflation is less firmly anchored at 2% than in the US or Europe, the Bank \"must respond swiftly to signs of an upward deviation in prices, including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\" and \"more swiftly and strongly than would be necessary if underlying inflation were anchored more firmly\"; what is required is \"not to go slowly, but unhurried haste\" (ゆっくりではなく、悠々として急ぐ); upside risks from Middle East raw-material costs, import prices on the weak yen and still-robust AI demand; within a few months of each hike the Bank can assess conditions from surveys and firms. Fisco quotes \"my view that neutral is around 2% is unchanged\"; Reuters says he did not mention the figure. Press conference 14:00 not reported at this refresh.","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/bojs-tamura-signals-chance-of-fasterpace-interest-rate-hikes-5051855"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The AI names that took 1,633 points off the index on 25 Nov gave 700 to 875 back on 26 Nov; the broad market rotated the other way. The first stabilisation test is 3.6% away on the Nikkei and 3.1% on TOPIX at the morning levels.","src":"OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045934; Fisco via Zaikei 879261; Kabutan 13:00 (n202611260631)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo 26 Nov morning: Nikkei 57,886.95 (+875.33, +1.54%) after opening 57,429.54 (+417.92) and rising more than 1,200 to the 58,200s in the 10:00 hour, where sellers appeared above 58,000; 57,708.53 (+696.91) at 13:00; TOPIX 3,772.98 (+26.78, +0.71%); Prime 863 up against 629 down at 13:00, 20 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil, electrical equipment, glass) against air transport, precision and food; Advantest 25,260 (+1,515, +6.4%, +327 points), SoftBank Group 3,000 (+303.5, +11.3%, +246), Tokyo Electron 8,029 (+204, +103), Kioxia 7,732 (+837, +12.1%), TDK +4.2%, Ibiden and Murata up; Fast Retailing 85,120 (−1,050, −85 points), Daikin −1.9%, food and retail names that rose on 25 Nov sold; December futures 57,960 (+920) at noon; morning turnover ¥3.59tn, thin before Thanksgiving.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045934_202611261157/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"For Japan the headline rate matches the July 2025 deal and autos are exempt, so the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect in 2027; the 20% alternative tied to the $550bn investment commitments is the new political risk. It pushed US futures down overnight before PCE.","src":"Wiley Rein alert; Baker McKenzie Global Import Blog; Reuters via Korea Times; BigGo Finance; coki.jp","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR (25 Nov, US time) issued affirmative Section 301 determinations on structural excess capacity and proposed tariffs on 14 economies: Japan 15% in total net of MFN and the existing 12.5% July 2026 tariff, with an alternative of up to 20% if consultations on the deal commitments are judged unsatisfactory; the EU 15% (alternative 20%), Korea and Taiwan 15% (25%), Switzerland 15% (20%), China up to 20% with Annex A goods 10% and Annex B exempt (up to 40% if engagement fails), Vietnam up to 35% (20% with an agreement), India up to 25% (17%), Singapore up to 20% (10%), Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand and Cambodia up to 18% (25%), Bangladesh up to 18% (20%); Mexico and Norway still under investigation. For the 13 non-China economies goods under Section 232 (autos and parts, steel, aluminium, copper, semiconductors, patented pharmaceuticals, wood) are exempt and the new rate is reduced by the forced-labour 301 duties, so the duties do not stack. Comments and hearing requests due 5 Jan 2027; hearing from 25 Jan 2027 at the ITC; final action expected early 2027. Greer: the caps match the deals already reached. The report cites Japan's industrial plans, BoJ-era financial repression, NISA, zombie firms, the technical-intern system and Rapidus and JASM subsidies (coki). No Japanese government reaction was found.","url":"https://www.wiley.law/alert-USTR-Announces-Affirmative-Section-301-Determinations-and-Proposes-New-Tariffs-on-14-Trading-Partners-to-Address-Structural-Excess-Capacity"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A late recovery after a morning liquidation in AI names, hot core PCE and the Section 301 proposal: yields fell anyway, which is the haven trade, and the Fed hike is fully priced. Tokyo took the recovery, not the fall, on 26 Nov.","src":"CappNotes closing look; Motley Fool; GSM Global; OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045891; Fisco via Zaikei 879162 (US data)","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 25 Nov: S&P 500 7,182.78 (−18.31, −0.25%) after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq 23,585.80 (−147.74, −0.62%) after −4.6%, Dow 50,182.95 (+164.25, +0.33%) after a fall of more than 1,100, Russell 2000 2,704.06 (+0.71%), VIX 28.29 (+7.3%) after topping 30; Nvidia $149.30 (−$6.08, −3.91%, low $141.17), AMD $406.03 (−1.75%), Micron −4.53%, Broadcom $300.86 (−0.07%), Anthropic $82.79 (+1.07%, under the $83 IPO), HP +11.49%; SOXX −0.50%, SMH −1.01% (SOX close not found). Data: October PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.8% y/y, core PCE +0.3% m/m and 3.3% y/y (consensus 3.2%, September 3.1%); Q3 GDP second estimate 4.3% annualized (consensus 3.5%, advance 3.4%) with consumption 4.2%; durable goods +0.2% (consensus +0.7%), ex-transport +0.5%; initial claims 189,000 (200,000); new home sales 701,000 (650,000); personal income +0.7%. US 2-year 4.61% (−2 bp), 10-year 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp; 4.79% low), 30-year 5.24%; CME FedWatch 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Gaitame 96.4%; Polymarket 94%, Kalshi 93%); dollar index 103.71 (−0.10%); WTI $83.68 (+1.99%), Brent $94.61 (+3.51%) after a Houthi strike on Ras Tanura; gold $3,534.70 (−0.71%), under $3,500 intraday for the first time since August 2025. US markets closed 26 Nov, early close 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/25/stock-market-today-nov-25-nvidia-slides-3-9-as-the-market-considers-hot-pce-inflation-data-and-new-tariff-proposals/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Bull-flattening to 20 years on the haven bid and a hold being priced at the front end, while the super-long end cheapened on supply: the two halves of the curve are now sending opposite signals and the 40-year at 4.18% is the fiscal gauge into the MoF meetings.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 879100; stock-marketdata.com; Newsquawk","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:36): 2-year 1.720% (−5.5 bp; Fisco 1.722%), 5-year 2.030% (−8 bp), 10-year 2.645% (−7.5 bp, lowest in about three weeks; Fisco 2.627%, stock-marketdata.com 2.620%), 20-year 3.580% (−4.5 bp; Fisco 3.556%), 30-year 3.950% (+1.5 bp), new 40-year 4.180% (+6 bp); December futures 128.24 (+64 sen), open 127.66, high 128.39 (highest since 6 Nov). The ¥300bn 40-year reopening (19th issue, 3.8% coupon, results 12:35) cleared at a 4.09% highest accepted yield, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September, called somewhat weak by a domestic strategist on investor demand; the new 40-year traded 4.145% before the sale. Drivers: the Nikkei down more than 2,000 intraday, AI selling on Nasdaq on 24 Nov, US 10-year under 4.90% in Tokyo hours. MoF primary-dealer meeting 26 Nov, investor meeting 27 Nov.","url":"https://www.zaikei.co.jp/article/20261125/879100.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The index fell 2.78% while 64% of the market rose: the drawdown is confined to the AI complex and the close held 575 points above the 20 Nov liquidation low. The posture stays Risk-off on the index, with breadth the best since the fall began.","src":"TBL Advisory 20261125nk; Tokyo Hodo Shimbun 30906; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045754; QUICK Money World 223679; Fisco via Zaikei 879109 (VI); Kabutan headline (night session)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 25 Nov: Nikkei 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%), open 58,383.49, low 56,502.97 (down more than 2,100 in the morning; morning close 56,710.43), Prime turnover ¥7.4977tn on 2,734m shares, Prime 993 up, 537 down, 22 flat (63.9% advancing), 23 of 33 sectors up: pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%, retail +2.95% against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48%, glass and ceramics −3.79%. Advantest about −762 index points and Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328), SoftBank Group −379 points (−14.8%, year-to-date low), Ibiden −115, Fujikura 2,062.5 (−14.7%, downgraded), Murata 4,633 (−14.2%); Fast Retailing 86,170 (+2,130, +172 points), Chugai 7,501 (+426, +43), Terumo up, Ono Pharmaceutical 2,699 (+10.7%) on an Amgen patent settlement. Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88, +5.97%; high 43.23). December futures 57,040 (−1,940); night session opened 56,660 and closed 57,370 (Kabutan headline). TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%), Growth 250 640.60 (−14.24). USD/JPY 159.21 to 159.23 at 17:00.","url":"https://tbladvisory.com/20261125nk/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Services prices are running well above 2% at the corporate level, which supports the BoJ's December case even as the market trims it to about 78%.","src":"Bank of Japan","tag":"Data","title":"October services PPI (BoJ, 08:50): +4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, m/m +1.1%; ex-international transport +3.2% (3.0%); international transport +99.1% (ocean freight +120.4%), transport and postal +9.4%, accommodation +12.1%, leasing and rental +11.8%, real estate +3.0%, information and communications +2.5%, advertising −1.2%.","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/statistics/pi/cspi_release/sppi2610.pdf"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday's Huang rally unwound in the chips and memory names that lead the Nikkei while the Dow rose; the rotation out of AI is the Tokyo driver on 25 Nov.","src":"Company Chronicle; Motley Fool; TradingKey; OANDA Japan NY digest","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 24 Nov: S&P 500 7,201.09 (−36.08, −0.50%), Nasdaq 23,733.54 (−356.02, −1.48%), Nasdaq 100 −1.99%, Dow 50,018.70 (+423.39, +0.85%), Russell 2000 2,684.94 (+0.57%), VIX 26.36; Philadelphia Semiconductor Index −7.88%; Nvidia $155.38 (−$9.67, −5.86%, more than −8% intraday, 379m shares; Bernstein upgrade to Outperform, $200), AMD $413.27 (−8.86%), Micron −10.97%, SanDisk −11.13%, HPE −16.31%, Broadcom $301.08 (−3.98%), Anthropic $81.91 (−6.5%, under the $83 IPO price, $75.61 low), Microsoft +0.7%, Apple +1.4%; Dell: AI orders $60.2bn, backlog $132.3bn, inventory nearly tripled; US 10-year 4.90% to 4.93%; gold $3,561.9 (−89.5); WTI $82.05 (+1.88).","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/24/stock-market-today-nov-24-nvidia-gives-back-usd240-billion-in-market-value-despite-wall-street-upgrade-signaling-29-upside/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The yen weakened on the day the Nikkei rose and stayed weak as it fell, with the front-end gap about 290 bp; December BoJ pricing is drifting lower without a BoJ word.","src":"OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045662; Fisco via Zaikei 878985; OANDA Japan FOMC preview; MogWatch","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 24 Nov: USD/JPY closed 159.26 in New York (OANDA, +0.24; Fisco 159.35) after a 159.58 high and 158.99 low; EUR/JPY 174.64 (+1.27), EUR/USD 1.0966 (+0.0063), dollar index low 103.75; US 10-year 4.93% (−1 bp; Company Chronicle 4.90%); November consumer confidence 103.3 (consensus 92.4, October 91.9), Richmond Fed 3 (−2), Case-Shiller +2.6% y/y, FHFA 0.0% m/m; CME FedWatch 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec, 4.2% for 50 bp, cumulative 50 bp by January 65.8% (OANDA, 24 Nov evening). BoJ OIS: MogWatch 78.2% for 18 Dec at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov (implied 1.445%), OANDA 78.7%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/20261124_fomc_dec_preview/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The first post-holiday close confirmed a 3.91% rebound that still failed the 60,030 and 3,895 lines, while bonds rallied with crude lower.","src":"News On Japan; ad-hoc-news/AP; Reuters via WHTC; Kyodo via OANDA Japan; Fisco via Zaikei 878940; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 24 Nov: Nikkei 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%), high 59,262.57, up more than 2,800 at one point (Jiji), Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn, 26 of 33 sectors up led by nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and glass; Advantest +12.5%, Tokyo Electron +7.7% (AP), Ibiden limit-up, Murata and Taiyo Yuden sharply higher, Baycurrent a record; Inpex, Kobe Bussan, Nippon Shinyaku, Dentsu and KDDI fell. TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), ending five straight falls. JGBs: 2-year 1.765% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.12%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp, 2.695% intraday), 20-year 3.63%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, December futures 127.60 (+60 sen, 127.15 to 127.84). Reuters poll (22 analysts, 16 to 24 Nov): Nikkei 65,000 by June 2027, 67,500 by end-2027, 69,000 by mid-2028.","url":"https://newsonjapan.com/article/151515.php"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Manufacturing keeps the regime in Expansion and the services slide is the first survey evidence of the equity fall reaching households; the composite at a six-month low with confidence at a 2020 low is the warn for the household leg.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; ActionForex; au.investing; IndexBox","tag":"Data","title":"November flash PMIs (S&P Global / au Jibun Bank, 08:30): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders solid, manufacturing export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018 on Asian semiconductor and electronics demand, employment up, outstanding work rising a twelfth month; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low; business confidence joint-lowest since August 2020; input costs an eight-month low, output charges the weakest since March. Fiddes: 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity'.","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The governance shock that drove the 20 Nov liquidation reversed in one session; the SEC and DOJ inquiries continue (Al Jazeera), so the AI chain's governance strand is open even as the price has recovered.","src":"Nvidia newsroom and SEC 8-K; Al Jazeera; Motley Fool; Fox Business; STL.News; TBL Advisory","tag":"Markets","title":"Nvidia's board 'voted unanimously' to reinstate Jensen Huang as president, CEO and director effective immediately, citing 'input from senior levels of the United States government', 'no national security risk' and satisfaction with his 'candor', with an independent-director investigation of the reported foreign-intelligence effort, a new National Security and Export Control Committee, a chief compliance officer search and continued cooperation with government investigations; Kress continues as CFO. Trump: 'the correct and proper decision', he was 'represented in the board meeting'. Nvidia $165.05 (+$39.52, +31.48%, range $156.93 to $171.50, 772.5m shares, about $3.03tn; largest one-day percentage gain in more than two decades per Motley Fool); S&P 500 7,237.17 (+326.78, +4.73%), Nasdaq 24,089.56 (+1,471.82, +6.51%), Dow 49,595.31 (+1,447.98, +3.01%), Russell 2000 2,669.66 (+3.40%), VIX 26.62 from 35.89; Micron +19%, AMD $453.46 (+6.85%), Broadcom $313.55 (+3.00%), Oracle +12.34%, Tesla +11.5%, Meta +6.6%, Anthropic $87.64 (−1.4%). Chicago Nikkei futures 57,960.","url":"https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-board-reinstates-jensen-huang-as-president-ceo-and-board-director"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The yen gave back 45 sen of its haven bid as equities rebounded; the rate gaps (about 286 bp at the front end) hold USD/JPY near 159 with 160 and the MoF 98 sen away, and December is still the base case on both sides.","src":"Reuters via Kitco; NY Fed Teller Window; Fox Business; Trading Economics; Fisco via Zaikei 878815; OANDA Japan NY digest; TBL Advisory; Minkabu FX","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 23 Nov: Williams (Bronx) said inflation is 'too high' and 'there is still work to do', has no particular timing in mind and 'would not exclude' moving at consecutive meetings, with PCE inflation about 3.5% this year, just above 2% next year and 2% in 2028, GDP growth about 2.75%; CME FedWatch 96% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Fox Business), Trading Economics about 93% with 50 bp largely ruled out, Bessent warning against excessive tightening. The $69bn 2-year auction stopped at 4.703% with 2.653 cover (six-auction average 2.63), a −0.3 bp tail and 51.5% indirects; the 10-year fell from 4.98% to 4.94% (flat on the day), the 2-year 4.683% (+3.6 bp). USD/JPY closed 159.02 in New York (+0.45) after a 159.50 high and a 158.87 low; EUR/JPY 173.37, lowest since October 2025; EUR/USD 1.0903 after 1.0881, lowest since April 2025; dollar index high 104.53, highest since March 2025; WTI $80.17 (−3.24), gold $3,651.4 (+136.0). Minkabu's weekly preview (22 Nov): December BoJ pricing about 81% after the CPI miss from about 88%; two hikes by March 47% from 54%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045448_202611240720/"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The liquidation read is confirmed on the index leg (close under 57,435 on 93% negative breadth and the heaviest turnover since 6 Nov) and not on the volatility leg (VI under 40); the first stabilisation test is 6% away on both index legs. Tokyo reopens 24 Nov with the flash PMIs and futures at 54,400.","src":"Fisco via Zaikei 878619; TBL Advisory 20261120nk; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045088 and the 20261120 explainer; stock-marketdata.com (VI); OANDA Japan NY digest (night futures)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close on 20 Nov: Nikkei 56,436.65 (−3,007.56, −5.06%), the fifth-largest point fall on record (after −4,451.28 on 5 Aug 2024, −3,836.48 on 20 Oct 1987, −3,643.13 on 6 Nov 2026 and −3,318 on 15 Oct 2026), the first close in the 56,000s in about seven months, 998 points under the 57,435 liquidation line and 22.0% below the 72,366.34 record; open 58,551.56, high 58,707.48, down more than 2,800 by 9:50, −2,198 at 10:25, 56,775.04 at the morning close, past −3,000 in the 14:40s, closed on the low at 15:30; week −7.7% from 61,142.35. TOPIX 3,674.22 (−148.25, −3.88%), a fifth fall; Growth 250 638.22 (−5.45%). Prime 90 up / 1,443 down (93%), turnover ¥8,638.9bn on 3,085.63m shares; 2 of 33 sectors up (insurance +0.65%, air transport +0.33%), nonferrous metals −9.90%, electrical equipment −5.70%, glass and ceramics −5.21%, machinery −4.99%, pharmaceuticals −0.58%. Advantest (−10.3%) and SoftBank Group (−14.5%) about 1,016 index points, top five about 1,527; Fujikura −12.2%, Kioxia −11.7%, Lasertec −9.2%, Disco −8.3%, Tokyo Electron −7.1%, TDK −6.9%, Mitsubishi Heavy −5.5%, Shin-Etsu −5.3%, Hitachi −4.7%, Toyota −2.0%, Fast Retailing −1.4%, Sony −1.2%; Tokio Marine +2.0%, Otsuka second-largest positive contributor. Nikkei VI 33.07 (+2.43). December futures 56,420 (−3,320); night session opened 56,760 and fell to 54,400 after Wall Street. USD/JPY 159.20 at 16:00 Tokyo. Fisco and OANDA cite Anthropic sell-the-news, the AMD short report, Fed hike pricing and the three-day weekend; Osaka holiday futures trading on 23 Nov is cancelled.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045088_202611201757/"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"At target and a touch under forecast: December stays priced (2-year 1.83% on the day) and the overshoot case is trimmed; the only caveat in the coverage is market instability, which the same morning's 4.5% equity fall makes the live one.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com and AOL; Trading Economics; ChainCatcher (headline); Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","tag":"Data","title":"October national CPI (MIC, 08:30): core (ex fresh food) +2.2% y/y against a 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and 2.1% in September, the highest since December 2025; core-core (ex fresh food and energy) +2.2% from 2.4%, a six-month high; headline +2.3% from 2.1%; food +2.2% from 2.9%, goods +3.2% from 2.8%, services +1.4%. Minami (Norinchukin): 'strong chance' of a December hike and another in March 2027 'when inflation is likely to exceed 3%'; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life): 'if markets remain unstable, the BOJ may prefer to stand pat next month'; economy minister Kiuchi: 'signs the economy is moving closer toward a desirable state'.","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japan-oct-core-cpi-rises-22-yryr-5040343"}],"headline":"Tokyo closed 25 Nov at 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%) with Advantest and Tokyo Electron about 1,328 of the points and SoftBank Group at a year-to-date low while 64% of Prime rose; TOPIX 3,746.20 (−0.50%). JGBs bull-flattened to the 20-year (2-year 1.720%, 10-year 2.645%, −7.5 bp, a three-week low) while the 30-year rose to 3.950% and the 40-year to 4.180% after a weak 40-year auction; USD/JPY closed 159.17 in New York. On 26 Nov Tamura said the Bank must bring the rate to neutral soon and may need to accelerate hikes, with December pricing at 76.7% before he spoke; the USTR proposed a 15% total Section 301 rate on Japan with autos exempt; the Nikkei was 57,886.95 (+1.54%) at the morning close on the same AI names.","marketClose":"Wed 25 Nov 2026","notes":["This refresh ran at about 05:10 UTC (14:10 JST) on Thursday 26 Nov, during the Tokyo afternoon session, so the Nikkei, TOPIX and JGB rows and the history hold the 25 Nov closes (marketClose); the 26 Nov morning-session levels, the Tamura speech and the USTR proposal are in the text only. The 26 Nov JGB morning quotes, the Tamura press conference (14:00), the MoF primary-dealer meeting readout and the September leading and coincident index revisions (14:00) were not available in any page opened.","Nikkei 25 Nov close 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%) is TBL Advisory, Tokyo Hodo Shimbun, OANDA Japan (DZH) and QUICK Money World, which agree; TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68) is OANDA Japan, TBL Advisory and Tokyo Hodo Shimbun. Breadth is 993 up / 537 down (TBL Advisory) against 991 / 533 (Tokyo Hodo Shimbun). The intraday high was not found (the open, 58,383.49, is the highest level reported). The night-session futures close 57,370 is a Kabutan headline only. Nikkei VI 33.39 is Fisco via Zaikei.","JGBs 25 Nov are QUICK/NQN closes via SMTB (2-year 1.720%, 5-year 2.030%, 10-year 2.645%, 20-year 3.580%, 30-year 3.950%, 40-year 4.180%, futures 128.24) and the changes are against the QUICK/NQN 24 Nov closes (1.775 / 2.110 / 2.720 / 3.625 / 3.935 / 4.120), which differ by up to 1 bp from the Trading Economics 24 Nov values used in the previous refresh (2-year 1.765%, 30-year 3.94%); the 2-year and 30-year rows now carry the QUICK basis as the prior. Fisco prints the 10-year at 2.627% and stock-marketdata.com 2.620%, so the 10-year sources span 2.5 bp. The 40-year auction consensus (4.070% to 4.080%) and the \"somewhat weak\" comment are QUICK/NQN; the average yield and tail were not found.","USD/JPY 159.17 for 25 Nov is OANDA Japan's New York digest close (−0.09); Kyodo gives 159.14 to 159.24 at 17:00 New York; the 159.41 high and 158.66 low are Gaitame (159.401 / 158.664) and Fisco via Zaikei. Wall Street 25 Nov closes are CappNotes, Motley Fool and GSM Global, which agree on the index levels; CappNotes is an AI-assisted recap. The SOX close for 25 Nov was not found (SOXX −0.50%, SMH −1.01% are the proxies). US data are Fisco via Zaikei.","Tamura: the English quotes are Reuters via Investing.com and AOL; the Japanese 悠々として急ぐ and 中立水準に早く are Kyodo via OANDA Japan and Fisco via Zaikei 879251. Fisco reports he said his view of neutral at about 2% is unchanged; Reuters says he did not mention the figure; both are recorded. BoJ December pricing 76.7% is MogWatch OIS as of 01:01 JST on 26 Nov, before the speech; no post-speech OIS reading was found. The USTR figures are the Wiley Rein and Baker McKenzie summaries of the Federal Register notices and Reuters via the Korea Times; the Japan 20% alternative is in the law-firm summaries, while BigGo says Japan \"will not exceed 15%\". Tokyo CPI consensus is Fisco via Zaikei and Minkabu. Calendar and next-release dates are Tokyo dates."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Risk-off. Tokyo closed 25 Nov at 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%) after opening 58,383.49 and touching 56,502.97 (down more than 2,100) in the morning; TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%). The fall was the AI complex: Advantest about −762 index points, Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328 points), SoftBank Group −379 (−14.8%, a year-to-date low), Ibiden −115, Fujikura −14.7%, Murata −14.2%, while Prime had 993 up against 537 down (63.9%), 23 of 33 sectors rose (pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%) against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48% and glass −3.79%, and Fast Retailing added 172 points; turnover ¥7.50tn on 2,734m shares; Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88) after a 43.23 intraday high; December futures 57,040 (−1,940), night session 57,370. The close held 575 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65), so no new drawdown low, and it is 5.0% under the 60,030 first-test line with TOPIX 3.8% under 3,895. Wall Street 25 Nov then erased most of a morning fall (S&P 500 7,182.78, −0.25%, after −3.2% intraday; Nasdaq 23,585.80, −0.62%, after −4.6%; Dow 50,182.95, +0.33%, after −1,100; Russell 2000 +0.71%; VIX 28.29 after topping 30; Nvidia $149.30, −3.91%, low $141.17; AMD −1.75%; Micron −4.53%; Anthropic $82.79, +1.07%) with core PCE 3.3% y/y and Q3 GDP revised to 4.3%. Tokyo 26 Nov (afternoon session at this refresh): opened 57,429.54 (+417.92), up more than 1,200 to the 58,200s in the 10:00 hour, morning close 57,886.95 (+875.33, +1.54%), 57,708.53 (+696.91) at 13:00; TOPIX 3,772.98 (+26.78, +0.71%); Prime 863 up against 629 down at 13:00, 20 of 33 sectors up; Advantest 25,260 (+1,515, +327 points), SoftBank Group 3,000 (+11.3%, +246), Tokyo Electron 8,029 (+2.6%, +103), Kioxia 7,732 (+12.1%); Fast Retailing −85 points; futures 57,960 (+920) at noon; morning turnover ¥3.59tn, thin before the US holiday. Under the framework the drawdown is 21.7% from the 72,831.73 record on the 25 Nov close, confined to the AI names on both legs (down 25 Nov, up 26 Nov), and the broad market has held for two sessions; that is a better reading than 20 Nov but not a stabilisation. The posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895 with the Nikkei VI under 35; a close under 56,436.65 is a new low for the drawdown."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"No new growth data since the November flash PMIs (24 Nov): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low. The leading set is still in expansion on the manufacturing leg: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, September core machinery orders +13.4% y/y with a +4.3% q/q Q4 plan, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. Coincident: October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11.77tn, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, industrial production +1.7%, real wages +2.2%, unemployment 2.4%, jobs ratio 1.19, retail +2.3%. Prices: October core CPI 2.2%, at target; October services PPI +4.2% y/y, the highest since March 1990. The USTR Section 301 proposal (25 Nov) puts Japan at 15% in total, net of MFN and the existing 12.5% tariff, with Section 232 goods (autos, steel, aluminium, semiconductors) exempt, comments to 5 Jan and a hearing from 25 Jan 2027, so the export leg is unchanged until a rate takes effect; the 20% alternative is the risk. The September leading and coincident index revisions (26 Nov, 14:00; consensus 116.4 and 119.5, unchanged from the preliminary) were not confirmed at this refresh. The framework reads leading over coincident; manufacturing is at the top of its five-year range and the regime stays Expansion with the household side the weak leg and the services PMI 0.5 point from the warn band. A services PMI under 50 in the final (3 Dec) or the December flash, consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves it to Slowdown."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.4","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":55.1},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Nov 26","v":53.7}],"latest":"53.7","name":"au Jibun Bank Manufacturing PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"53.3","read":"The November flash rose to 53.7 from 53.3 in October (consensus 53.5), the first rise since the 54.9 of August. Output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders grew at a solid pace and export orders had their second-fastest growth since January 2018 on semiconductor and electronics demand from other Asian economies; employment rose, led by manufacturing, and outstanding work grew for a twelfth month. Input cost inflation slowed to an eight-month low and output charges to the weakest since March. Business confidence across both sectors fell to its joint-lowest since August 2020, so the order books are strong and the mood is not. Well above 50 and at the top of the five-year range: good. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, chip-led","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex, au.investing and IndexBox (24 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Up; beat 53.5","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"change":"−1.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":51},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":52.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":50.5}],"latest":"50.5","name":"au Jibun Bank Services PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"51.7","read":"The November flash fell to 50.5 from 51.7, the lowest since the 50.0 of May, with new business growth at a four-month low; the composite fell to 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low, and activity still expanded for a twentieth month. Business confidence is at its joint-lowest since August 2020, with manufacturers more optimistic than services firms; S&P Global's Fiddes said 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity' and that 'uncertainty remains elevated'. This is the first survey taken through the equity fall and the first sign of it in the household-facing data. Still above 50 so the signal stays good by the 50 line, but it is 0.5 point from the warn band and the divergence with manufacturing (53.7) is the widest since the spring; the final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Just above 50; composite six-month low","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and au.investing (24 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"New business four-month low","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japans-factory-activity-expands-in-november-pmi-shows-5046775"},{"change":"+2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":12},{"l":"Mar 24","v":11},{"l":"Jun 24","v":13},{"l":"Sep 24","v":13},{"l":"Dec 24","v":14},{"l":"Mar 25","v":12},{"l":"Jun 25","v":13},{"l":"Sep 25","v":14},{"l":"Dec 25","v":15},{"l":"Mar 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24}],"latest":"+24","name":"Tankan Large Manufacturers DI","next":"mid-Dec (Dec survey)","period":"Sep 2026 survey","prior":"+22","read":"Released 1 Oct. Large manufacturers improved for a sixth quarter to +24, the best since March 2018, while large non-manufacturers slipped to +35 from +37. The December outlook DI is +21 for manufacturers and +30 for non-manufacturers, so firms expect some cooling. FY2026 capex plans for large firms are +11.3%. The monthly Reuters Tankan (11 Nov, survey 27 Oct to 6 Nov) points the other way for December: manufacturers +27 from +22 (consensus +23), the highest since August 2021, non-manufacturers +35 from +23, the highest since June 2018, with electronics +39 on memory-chip demand and the February outlook +28 and +29; respondents cited rising prices and interest rates as the drag. The December BoJ Tankan is due mid-December.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = favorable equals unfavorable","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2018","src":"Bank of Japan; Reuters Tankan via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/tk/yoshi/tk2609.htm"},{"change":"−0.4","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":37.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":37.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":37.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":40},{"l":"Mar 26","v":33.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":32.2},{"l":"May 26","v":33.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":33.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":34.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":35.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":35.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":35}],"latest":"35.0","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"2 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"35.4","read":"October fell to 35.0 from 35.4, a touch under the 35.1 consensus and the lowest since July, with every component lower: livelihood 33.2 (33.8), willingness to buy durables 26.8 (27.3), income growth 40.4 (40.5), employment 39.4 (40.0). The index has been in the 32 to 36 band since the March drop from 40.0 when the Hormuz closure lifted fuel prices. The October Eco Watchers survey (10 Nov) was also soft: the current index fell to 46.1 from 47.0 (consensus 46.8), households 45.5 (−0.9), corporate 47.6 (+0.5), and the outlook index fell to 46.5 from 47.4. Energy prices turned positive again in the Tokyo CPI in October, which explains the slip; the November reading prints on 2 Dec.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = neutral line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 40 band","src":"Cabinet Office via Trading Economics; Eco Watchers via RTTNews, FX.co and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lowest since July","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−5.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":12.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-6.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":16.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":24.7},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":15.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":11.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":13.4}],"latest":"+13.4%","name":"Core Machinery Orders, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+18.6%","read":"Released 19 Nov. Core orders (private, ex ships and electric power) fell 0.3% on the month to ¥1,066.2bn after +5.2% in August (Newsquawk consensus −0.5%, RTTNews +1.3%), the first fall in two months, and rose 13.4% from a year earlier against +13.7% expected and +18.6% in August. Manufacturers' orders rose 3.9% m/m to ¥547.6bn and non-manufacturers' ex utilities 1.1% to ¥547.2bn; government orders fell 1.3%, overseas orders rose 6.2%, total orders were flat. The Cabinet Office kept its 'signs of a pickup' assessment and the Q4 (Oct to Dec) forecast is +4.3% q/q for core orders, +2.6% for manufacturers, +5.2% for non-manufacturers and +8.8% for total orders. The series is noisy; the Tankan capex plans (+11.3% for large firms) and October machine tool orders (+42.5% y/y, the sixteenth straight gain) are the cleaner reads and point the same way. A double-digit y/y gain with a positive Q4 plan keeps the capex lead in place; the heat has come off the August beat.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Small miss; Q4 plan +4.3% q/q","src":"Cabinet Office via FX.co, RTTNews, Newsquawk, Kyodo (OANDA Japan) and Japan Planners (Q4 forecast)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, still double-digit","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3299479"},{"change":"−8.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-4.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-29.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":11.4},{"l":"May 26","v":33.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-2.7}],"latest":"−2.7%","name":"Housing Starts, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+6.1%","read":"Housing starts fell 2.7% y/y in September against a +2.9% consensus, the first fall since March after five positive months (+33.9% in May to +6.1% in August). Built-for-sale −4.5%, owned −3.4%, rented −1.6%, issued +18.4%. The comparison month, September 2025, was itself −7.3%, so the level is weak rather than flattered by the base. With the 10-year JGB near 2.8% and mortgage rates rising after three BoJ hikes, the housing leg of the leading set has turned down.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Falls on a weak base, missed","src":"MLIT via RTTNews and Newsquawk","trend":"down","trendWord":"Turns negative","url":"https://www.rttnews.com/3706092/japan-housing-starts-fall-2-7-in-september.aspx"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−1.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":-2.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.7}],"latest":"+1.7%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct prelim)","period":"Sep 2026 (final)","prior":"+2.7%","read":"The September final (METI, 16 Nov) revised output to +2.0% m/m from the +2.2% preliminary and to +1.7% y/y from +1.9% (consensus +1.9%), after −2.4% m/m in August; a ninth positive y/y month, slower on the base. The preliminary detail stands: eight of fifteen sectors rose, led by motor vehicles, production machinery and general-purpose and business-oriented machinery, with iron and steel down; shipments +0.6%, inventories +2.5%. METI's survey projects +5.1% in October and +0.8% in November, so Q4 starts with output catching up to the order book even as Q3 GDP fell 0.1% annualized on consumption and imports. October preliminary is due 30 Nov.","signal":"good","signalWord":"m/m rebound held at +2.0%","src":"METI final via Newsquawk and FX.co (16 Nov); preliminary via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Revised down","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japanese-industrial-production-final-sep-yy-17-prev-27"},{"change":"−0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.1},{"l":"May 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.3}],"latest":"+2.3%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+2.9%","read":"September rose 2.3% y/y against a 3.3% consensus (Trading Economics 3.8%) and fell 0.7% m/m after −1.2% in August; August was revised up to +2.9% from +2.7%. A seventh straight y/y gain, but the slowest since June and the second miss in a row. The household survey for September (10 Nov) was worse: spending fell 6.8% y/y against −3.1% and 2.8% m/m against +0.3%, the tenth straight annual fall and the steepest since September 2020, which the ministry blamed on rain every day of the month in Tokyo; the survey is small and volatile. With headline CPI at 2.1% in September, real retail growth is near zero and household spending is negative in real terms, consistent with confidence at 35.0.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; household spending −6.8%","src":"METI via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/retail-sales-annual"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.5},{"l":"May 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4}],"latest":"2.4%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"2.5%","read":"September fell to 2.4% against a 2.5% consensus, matching July's 12-month low. Unemployed fell 100K to 1.67mn, employment rose 340K to 68.68mn, a four-month high, and the labour force rose 250K to 70.34mn. The range since 2022 is 2.4% to 2.8%, so the labour market is at the tight end of it.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tight","src":"MIC via FX.co; history FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back to the low","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3259063"},{"change":"+0.01","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.19},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.18},{"l":"May 26","v":1.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.19}],"latest":"1.19","name":"Jobs-to-Applicants Ratio","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"1.18","read":"Rose to 1.19 from 1.18 (consensus 1.18), the first increase since June and the highest since December, but still below the 1.2 level the framework treats as tight and down from 1.35 in late 2022. With unemployment at 2.4% and employment at a four-month high, labour demand firmed in September; the Tankan employment DI (−38 all firms) says shortages remain severe.","ref":1,"refLabel":"1.0 = one opening per applicant","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1.2 line","src":"MHLW via FX.co","trend":"up","trendWord":"First rise since Jun","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3258824"},{"change":"+3.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":16.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":11.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.8},{"l":"May 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":20.5}],"latest":"+20.5%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"+16.7%","read":"October exports rose 20.5% y/y to ¥11,768.8bn, the highest monthly value on record and the 14th straight gain, against a +20.0% consensus and September's +16.7% (Trading Economics shows September revised to +16.5%). Export volume rose 5.8%, so the gain is not only price. Semiconductors +49.7% by value, electrical machinery +33.6%, machinery +21.2%, transport equipment +18.0% with motor vehicles +19.9%; by destination the US +23.9% (motor vehicles to the US +37.2%, surplus ¥690.6bn), Asia +22.0%, the EU +22.1%, China +13.3% (chip equipment to China +42.6%, motor vehicles to China −53.3%), the Middle East −15.0%. Imports rose 21.6% to ¥12.25tn against about +25.8% expected, on mineral fuels +36.5% and petroleum +60.5% by value for 0.7% less volume (crude from the US +738% by volume, from the Middle East −16.5%); the deficit was ¥476.5bn against ¥955bn expected, a sixth monthly deficit. External demand is the strongest leg of the economy and the chip chain is its whole engine; the US tariff deal is not showing in autos to the US (+37.2%). Next: November trade in mid-December.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Beat; chips +49.7%, volume +5.8%","src":"MoF trade statistics (provisional) via Xinhua, ActionForex, IndexBox and Trading Economics; Newsquawk (consensus); Zaikei (QUICK consensus)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Record, 14th gain","url":"https://english.news.cn/asiapacific/20261118/a9e3802d03274a27b6508b13f7aa7ff5/c.html"},{"change":"+0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"+2.2%","name":"Real Wages, y/y","next":"7–8 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.6%","read":"September real wages rose 2.2% y/y, a ninth straight gain and above the 1.3% consensus, after August was revised to +1.6% from +1.5%. Nominal cash earnings rose 4.6% to ¥311,409, the fastest since January 1997 and above the 3.8% consensus, an eighth month above 3%; August was revised to 3.9%. Base pay rose 3.7% (3.8% in August), same-sample full-time base pay 3.1% (2.9%) and overtime 3.6%; special payments jumped 32.6%, which the ministry attributes to a statistical quirk, so the headline overstates the trend. The deflator CPI was 2.4% (2.2%). The BoJ's virtuous-cycle test is met on base pay but not yet on spending: September household spending fell 6.8% y/y (10 Nov), so the income gain is being saved or lost to weather. The Summary of Opinions has one member expecting next year's wage growth to be consistent with 2% and another expecting little change from this year. October wages print 7–8 Dec.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = wages keep pace with prices","signal":"good","signalWord":"Positive 9 months","src":"MHLW via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Faster growth","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/japans-real-wages-rise-for-ninth-month-in-september-backing-boj-rate-hike-5006105"},{"change":"−1.5 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 24","v":-0.4},{"l":"Jun 24","v":-0.5},{"l":"Sep 24","v":3.4},{"l":"Dec 24","v":1.6},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2},{"l":"Jun 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-0.1}],"latest":"−0.1%","name":"Real GDP, q/q annualized","next":"8 Dec (Q3 2nd prelim)","period":"Q3 2026 (1st prelim)","prior":"+1.4%","read":"Real GDP fell 0.02% q/q and 0.1% annualized in July to September (first preliminary, 16 Nov), against a +0.1% q/q and about +0.3% annualized consensus (range −0.3% to +1.5%), the first contraction in four quarters; Q2 was revised to +0.4% q/q and +1.4% annualized. Private consumption fell 0.3% q/q for a second quarter (consensus −0.2%) on food and household goods, weather hits to clothing and lodging, and payback from pre-tax-change purchases of air conditioners and cars; housing −1.1%, government consumption −0.0% (first negative in six quarters), public investment −1.1%, exports −0.2% with R&D services down, imports +2.5% (crude re-sourced after Hormuz), so net exports took 0.6 pt while domestic demand added 0.6 pt. Business investment rose 1.6% q/q (+6.8% annualized, consensus +1.5%) on AI servers and chip equipment, the first rise in three quarters. Nominal GDP +0.4% q/q and +1.6% annualized; deflator +2.4% y/y. Analysts (Meiji Yasuda, Daiwa) read the consumption weakness as price-driven and no bar to a December hike; Mizuho's Katagi says the board split sharpens if it persists. The second estimate is 8 Dec, after the MoF corporate survey on capex.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%","src":"Cabinet Office ESRI first preliminary (16 Nov) via Admiral Markets, Seoul Economic Daily and Yahoo Finance; Mace News consensus","trend":"down","trendWord":"First fall in 4 quarters","url":"https://admiralmarkets.com/analytics/traders-blog/japan-gdp-q3-2026-yen-usdjpy-160"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.4},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core CPI (ex fresh food), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.1%","read":"October core CPI rose 2.2% from 2.1%, under the 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and the highest since December 2025, a second month at or above the 2% target. Headline was 2.3% (September 2.1%); food inflation slowed to 2.2% from 2.9% while goods rose 3.2% (2.8%) and services 1.4%, so the mix is energy and goods rather than demand. With September real wages at +2.2% the reading is at target rather than squeezing consumption, and it leaves December intact: Minami (Norinchukin) expects a hike next month and another in March 2027 when inflation is likely to exceed 3%; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life) says only market instability could make the Bank stand pat. Tokyo CPI for November prints 27 Nov; national November CPI prints 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target; missed 2.3%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics, ChainCatcher (headline), Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Dec 2025","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core-core CPI (ex food and energy), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.4%","read":"Core-core CPI eased to 2.2% from September's 2.4%, a six-month high, so the demand-driven measure the BoJ watches is back at target. Goods prices rose 3.2% y/y (2.8% in September) and services 1.4% (1.5%), so the acceleration is in goods, food ex fresh slowed to 2.2% from 2.9%, and services inflation is still short of 2%. Tokyo core-core was 2.6% in October. The print trims the overshoot case Ueda made on 19 Nov (underlying inflation 'could run above 2%') without removing December; the pace argument for 2027 is what it bears on.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2%","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target, services 1.4%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Eased from 6-month high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.5},{"l":"May 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6}],"latest":"2.6%","name":"Tokyo Core CPI, y/y","next":"27 Nov (Nov; core consensus 2.9%)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.7%","read":"October core eased to 2.6% from 2.7%, matching the 2.6% consensus, the first deceleration in five months, while headline rose to 2.8% (consensus 2.7%) on fresh food (+8.6%) and energy, which turned to +2.2% y/y from −1.9% as electricity (+1.7%) and city gas (+4.9%) lost subsidy support. Ex fresh food and energy slowed to 2.6% from 2.9% (consensus 2.7%); services 2.2% (2.3%), goods 3.5% (3.2%), food ex fresh 3.0% (3.6%), accommodation 6.7%. Core m/m +0.3% after +0.9%. Above target on every measure but the underlying pace cooled; national October CPI prints on 20 Nov (core consensus 2.1%).","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Energy back, above 2%","src":"MIC via ActionForex, investingLive and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"First dip in 5 months","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/659199-tokyo-core-cpi-eases-to-2-6-but-energy-prices-revive-inflation-risks/"},{"change":"0 bp (8–1)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.75},{"l":"May 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.25},{"l":"25 Nov","v":1.25}],"latest":"1.25%","name":"BoJ Policy Rate","next":"18 Dec","period":"Held 30 Oct; 2-year 1.720% on 25 Nov","prior":"1.25%","read":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1: Takata dissented for 1.50%. October core CPI 2.2% (20 Nov) is at target; October services PPI +4.2% y/y (25 Nov), the fastest since March 1990, is the services-price print the Bank cites for a hike. Tamura (Kofu, 26 Nov) said the policy rate must be brought to neutral soon because financial conditions are still accommodative, that the Bank must respond swiftly to upside price signs \"including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\", and that the requirement is unhurried haste (悠々として急ぐ); Fisco quotes neutral at about 2% unchanged, Reuters says he did not restate it; the press conference was not reported at this refresh. The market has nonetheless trimmed December: MogWatch OIS 76.7% at 01:01 JST on 26 Nov (78.2% on 25 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.442% for 18 Dec and 1.603% for March, with 1.75% or higher by March at 45%; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. The 2-year closed 1.720% on 25 Nov, the first close under 1.75% since 10 Nov, and the ¥300bn 40-year auction cleared at 4.09% on 2.63 cover, above consensus and called somewhat weak. The board is split on the end-point: Tamura about 2%, Takata the middle of 1.1% to 2.5%, Koeda clearly above 1%, Asada and Sato limited room; Ueda speaks in Nagoya on 30 Nov. Under the framework the stance is Tightening with the pace now disputed between the market and the hawks: a hike on 18 Dec to 1.50% is still the base case, and the equity drawdown and the weak super-long demand are the two things that could hold it.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening; Dec pricing about 77%","src":"Bank of Japan (schedule, speeches page, services PPI); Reuters via Investing.com and AOL, Kyodo via OANDA Japan, Fisco via Zaikei 879251 (Tamura); MogWatch (OIS 26 Nov); QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2-year, 40-year auction); Reuters via Investing.com (poll)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Hawkish hold","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/bojs-tamura-signals-chance-of-fasterpace-interest-rate-hikes-5051855"},{"change":"+¥1.2tn in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":697.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":677.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":682.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":683.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":663},{"l":"Apr 26","v":663.3},{"l":"May 26","v":664.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":639.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":644.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":644.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":625},{"l":"Oct 26","v":626.2}],"latest":"¥626tn","name":"BoJ Total Assets","next":"early Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"¥625tn","read":"Total assets were ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct (BoJ accounts, published 4 Nov), up ¥1.2tn from ¥625.0tn at 30 Sep, with JGB holdings ¥505.3tn (+¥1.3tn): purchases of about ¥2.3tn a month this quarter outran light October redemptions. The balance sheet is down from a ¥765tn peak in Aug 2024 as JGB purchases are cut by about ¥200bn a quarter, heading to ¥2tn a month from April 2027; the June plan targets JGB holdings of about ¥480tn by March 2027. Quarter-end months dip on redemptions (September −¥20tn), so the October uptick is the between-quarter pattern, not a change in policy; ETF sales of about ¥860bn a year start in January.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT by taper","src":"Bank of Japan accounts (31 Oct and 30 Sep 2026); BoJ purchase plan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat after Q3 dip","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/boj/other/acmai/release/2026/ac261031.htm"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"−7.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.06},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.24},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.11},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.345},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.515},{"l":"May 26","v":2.65},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.67},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.79},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.063},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.77},{"l":"25 Nov","v":2.645}],"latest":"2.645%","name":"10-Year JGB Yield","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"2.720% on 24 Nov","read":"The 10-year closed 25 Nov at 2.645% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, −7.5 bp, the lowest in about three weeks; Fisco 2.627%, stock-marketdata.com 2.620%, so the sources span 2.5 bp), as the Nikkei fell more than 2,000 intraday and US yields fell below 4.90% during Tokyo hours; December futures closed 128.24 (+64 sen) after a 128.39 high, the highest since 6 Nov, in a 127.66 to 128.39 range. The belly led (5-year 2.030%, −8 bp; 20-year 3.580%, −4.5 bp) while the 30- and 40-year rose after the 40-year auction, so the curve bull-flattened to 20 years and steepened beyond. The US 10-year closed 4.85% on 25 Nov (−6 to −8 bp) after core PCE 3.3% and GDP 4.3%, so the gap is about 220 bp. Under the framework the 10-year is at the bottom of its November range (2.62% to 2.92%) on the haven bid and the fiscal signal sits in the 30- and 40-year rows; Tamura on 26 Nov and the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov are the next tests.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Haven bid","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (2.645%, −7.5 bp, curve, futures); Fisco via Zaikei 879100 (2.627%, futures range); stock-marketdata.com (2.620%); CappNotes and OANDA Japan NY digest (US 10-year)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"−5.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"6 Nov","v":1.73},{"l":"9 Nov","v":1.744},{"l":"10 Nov","v":1.761},{"l":"11 Nov","v":1.855},{"l":"13 Nov","v":1.88},{"l":"16 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"17 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"18 Nov","v":1.86},{"l":"19 Nov","v":1.845},{"l":"20 Nov","v":1.82},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.765},{"l":"25 Nov","v":1.72}],"latest":"1.720%","name":"2-Year JGB Yield","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"1.775% on 24 Nov (QUICK/NQN)","read":"The 2-year closed 25 Nov at 1.720% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, −5.5 bp from its 1.775% 24 Nov close; Fisco 1.722%), the first close under 1.75% since 10 Nov, with the 5-year at 2.030% (−8 bp). December pricing fell with it: MogWatch OIS 76.7% for 18 Dec (implied 1.442%) at 01:01 JST on 26 Nov against 78.2% on 25 Nov and 84.7% on 20 Nov, the March implied rate 1.603% (1.609%), 1.75% or higher by March 45%; the Reuters poll still has 86% of economists for 1.50%. Tamura then said on 26 Nov that the rate must reach neutral soon and the pace may need to accelerate; no 26 Nov close was available at this refresh. Under the framework a close under 1.75% says a hold is being priced in part, and the conflict is now between the market and the Bank's hawks: the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) and Ueda on 30 Nov decide it; a close back above 1.85% reprices December.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Hold being priced","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (1.720%, −5.5 bp; 24 Nov 1.775%); Fisco via Zaikei 879100 (1.722%); MogWatch (OIS 26 Nov); Reuters via Investing.com (poll, Tamura)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"+1.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"6 Nov","v":3.83},{"l":"9 Nov","v":3.85},{"l":"10 Nov","v":3.865},{"l":"11 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"13 Nov","v":4},{"l":"16 Nov","v":4.03},{"l":"17 Nov","v":4.04},{"l":"18 Nov","v":4.045},{"l":"19 Nov","v":3.99},{"l":"20 Nov","v":3.975},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"25 Nov","v":3.95}],"latest":"3.950%","name":"30-Year JGB Yield","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"3.935% on 24 Nov (QUICK/NQN)","read":"The 30-year closed 25 Nov at 3.950% (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, +1.5 bp from 3.935%) and the new 40-year at 4.180% (+6 bp) while every tenor to 20 years fell 4.5 to 8 bp: the super-long end sold off after the ¥300bn 40-year reopening cleared at a 4.09% highest yield, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September, which QUICK called somewhat weak on investor demand; the new 40-year traded 4.145% before the sale. The MoF primary-dealer meeting on 2027 issuance was held 26 Nov (no readout at this refresh) and the investor meeting follows 27 Nov; the 30-year auction is 3 Dec. Under the framework super-long yields rising on a day the belly rallied is supply and term premium, not policy, and with the yen at 159.17 the warn pair stays on; a 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal, 4.30% the fiscal line, and a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the pair off.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Weak auction","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB (30-year 3.950%, 40-year 4.180%, auction result and comment, 24 Nov baseline); Fisco via Zaikei 879100 (curve); Newsquawk (auction cover)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Higher","url":"https://fund.smtb.jp/smtbhp/qsearch.exe?F=mkt_news&KEY2=3217%2C3218"},{"change":"−2.78%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50339.48},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53322.85},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58850.27},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51063.72},{"l":"Apr 26","v":59284.92},{"l":"May 26","v":66329.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":70062.32},{"l":"Jul 26","v":64362.02},{"l":"Aug 26","v":66311.93},{"l":"Sep 26","v":66753.72},{"l":"Oct 26","v":63290.11},{"l":"25 Nov","v":57011.62}],"latest":"57,011.62","name":"Nikkei 225","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"58,644.63 on 24 Nov","read":"The Nikkei closed 25 Nov at 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%) after opening 58,383.49 and falling to 56,502.97 (down more than 2,100) in the morning; Prime turnover ¥7.50tn on 2,734m shares. The fall was four names: Advantest about −762 index points and Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328 points), SoftBank Group −379 (−14.8%, a year-to-date low) and Ibiden −115, with Fujikura −14.7% and Murata −14.2%, while 993 Prime stocks rose against 537, 23 of 33 sectors gained (pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%) and Fast Retailing (86,170, +2,130) added 172 points. Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88) after a 43.23 spike; December futures 57,040 (−1,940), night session 57,370. The close is 575 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65), 5.0% under the 60,030 first-test line and 21.7% under the 72,831.73 June record. Wall Street then recovered most of a 3% to 4.6% intraday fall on 25 Nov (S&P 500 −0.25%, Nasdaq −0.62%, Dow +0.33%, Nvidia −3.91%) and Tokyo on 26 Nov opened 57,429.54, rose more than 1,200 in the 10:00 hour, closed the morning at 57,886.95 (+875.33, +1.54%) and was 57,708.53 at 13:00 on Advantest (+327 points), SoftBank Group (+11.3%), Tokyo Electron and Kioxia (+12.1%), with Prime 863 up against 629 and turnover thin before the US holiday. Under the framework the index is a two-way market in the AI complex over a broad market that has risen two sessions running; no new drawdown low, no stabilisation. The Reuters poll (22 analysts, 16 to 24 Nov) has 65,000 by June 2027.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"TBL Advisory 20261125nk and Tokyo Hodo Shimbun 30906 (close, open, low, breadth, sectors, contributors, turnover); OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045754 (close, TOPIX, futures, SoftBank Group); QUICK Money World (close); Fisco via Zaikei 879109 (Nikkei VI); Kabutan headline (night futures); OANDA Japan mn_1045934, Fisco via Zaikei 879261 and Kabutan 13:00 (26 Nov morning)","trend":"down","trendWord":"AI names","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045754_202611251753/"},{"change":"−0.50%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 24","v":2784.92},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2658.73},{"l":"Jun 25","v":2852.84},{"l":"Sep 25","v":3137.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3408.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3497.86},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3994},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4108.65},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3965.07},{"l":"25 Nov","v":3746.2}],"latest":"3,746.20","name":"TOPIX","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"3,764.88 on 24 Nov","read":"TOPIX closed 25 Nov at 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%) against the Nikkei −2.78%: Prime 993 up, 537 down, 22 flat (63.9% advancing), 23 of 33 sectors up with pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53% and retail +2.95% against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48% and glass −3.79%; Growth 250 640.60 (−14.24). On 26 Nov it was 3,772.98 (+26.78, +0.71%) at the morning close with 20 of 33 sectors up and 863 Prime names up against 629 at 13:00. The 25 Nov close was 3.8% under the 3,895 first-test line, 8.8% under the 4,108.65 September close and 72 points above the 20 Nov low close of 3,674.22. Under the framework TOPIX down half a point on a 2.78% Nikkei fall, with 64% of Prime rising, says the drawdown is the AI leaders and the broad market is being bought; that is the best breadth read since the fall began and it is still not a stabilisation until 3,895.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045754, TBL Advisory 20261125nk and Tokyo Hodo Shimbun 30906 (25 Nov close, breadth, sectors); OANDA Japan mn_1045934 and Kabutan 13:00 (26 Nov morning); kabutore.biz quarterly closes","trend":"down","trendWord":"Breadth held","url":"https://tbladvisory.com/20261125nk/"},{"change":"−0.09","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":156.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":154.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":155.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":159.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":160.2},{"l":"May 26","v":159.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":161.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":160.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":160.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":157.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":157.55},{"l":"25 Nov","v":159.17}],"latest":"159.17","name":"USD/JPY","period":"25 Nov (New York close)","prior":"159.26 on 24 Nov","read":"USD/JPY closed 25 Nov in New York at 159.17 (OANDA Japan digest, −0.09; Kyodo 159.14 to 159.24 at 17:00 New York, 6 sen stronger yen), with a 159.41 high after 23:00 JST and a 158.66 low before 02:00 JST when the S&P 500 was down 3.2% and the Nasdaq 4.6% intraday; the yen was sold back as US stocks recovered. The US 10-year fell to 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp) even with core PCE at 3.3% y/y and Q3 GDP revised to 4.3%; CME 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec. Tokyo 26 Nov: 159.12 at 8:00, a 159.24 high before 9:00, a 158.95 low in the 9:00 hour before Tamura spoke at 10:00, back to 159.17 at noon with the Nikkei up 1.5% (Fisco: the equity rise strengthened yen selling); EUR/JPY 174.55 at noon (174.37 to 174.76), EUR/USD 1.0966. No MoF or Katayama comment dated 25 or 26 Nov was found; US markets are closed 26 Nov and close early 27 Nov; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov. Under the framework the pair is in the intervention zone with the front-end gap about 289 bp (US 2-year 4.61%, JGB 1.720%), the haven bid shows only on broad US falls (158.66), and a hawkish Tamura did not move it; 160 is 83 sen away and the carry-unwind path (155 to 156) needs the equity fall to spread beyond the AI names.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near 160","src":"OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045891 (159.17 close, low, EUR/JPY, EUR/USD, US 10-year, WTI, gold); Fisco via Zaikei 879162 and Gaitame (159.41 high, 158.66 low); Kyodo via OANDA Japan kn_2026112601000148 (17:00 New York); OANDA Japan mn_1045900 and mn_1045936, Fisco via Zaikei 879208 and 879251, Myforex DZH (Tokyo 26 Nov); CappNotes (US 2-year, FedWatch)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Flat","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045891_202611260720/"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closing levels for Wednesday 4 Nov unless a date is shown; 5 Nov figures are intraday.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Leading indicators: no new print. The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 (October 53.3, consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 (51.7) with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low. Standing: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. The September leading and coincident index revisions (14:00 on 26 Nov; consensus 116.4 and 119.5, both unchanged from the 9 Nov preliminary) were not confirmed at this refresh. Consumer confidence prints 2 Dec, the manufacturing final 1 Dec, the services final 3 Dec.","Coincident data: no new print. October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11,768.8bn; Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized (consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%; second estimate 8 Dec); industrial production +1.7% y/y; real wages +2.2% (nominal +4.6%); unemployment 2.4%; jobs ratio 1.19; retail sales +2.3%. The USTR (25 Nov) proposed Section 301 tariffs on 14 economies for structural excess capacity: Japan 15% in total, net of MFN and the existing 12.5% tariff, with an alternative of up to 20% if consultations are judged unsatisfactory; China up to 20% (40% if engagement fails), the EU 15% (20%), Korea and Taiwan 15% (25%), Vietnam up to 35%, India up to 25%; Section 232 goods (autos and parts, steel, aluminium, copper, semiconductors, pharmaceuticals, wood) exempt for the 13 non-China economies; comments due 5 Jan 2027, hearing from 25 Jan 2027, final action early 2027; Greer said the caps match the deals already reached; no Japanese government reaction was found. October industrial production, labour, retail and housing starts print 30 Nov. With core CPI at 2.2% and real wages at +2.2% the virtuous-cycle test is still passed.","Prices and policy: Tamura in Kofu (26 Nov, 10:00) said the Bank \"needs to bring its policy rate to a level deemed neutral to the economy soon\" because financial conditions remain accommodative, \"must respond swiftly to signs of an upward deviation in prices, including by accelerating the pace of policy interest rate hikes\", and that the requirement is \"not slowly, but unhurried haste\" (悠々として急ぐ); he cited Middle East raw-material costs, the weak yen and AI demand as upside risks and said underlying inflation is less firmly anchored than in the US or Europe; Fisco quotes him keeping neutral at about 2%, Reuters says he did not restate it; the 14:00 press conference was not reported at this refresh. December pricing before the speech: MogWatch OIS 76.7% at 01:01 JST on 26 Nov (78.2% on 25 Nov, 84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.442%, March 1.603% with 1.75% or higher at 45%; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. JGBs closed 25 Nov (QUICK/NQN): 2-year 1.720% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.030% (−8 bp), 10-year 2.645% (−7.5 bp, about a three-week low; Fisco 2.627%), 20-year 3.580% (−4.5 bp), 30-year 3.950% (+1.5 bp), 40-year 4.180% (+6 bp), futures 128.24 (+64 sen, high 128.39, highest since 6 Nov). The 40-year reopening cleared at 4.09%, above the 4.070% to 4.080% consensus, on 2.63 cover (September 3.10), and the new 40-year traded 4.145% before the sale and 4.180% at the close. US: October core PCE 3.3% y/y (consensus 3.2%), headline 3.8%, Q3 GDP 4.3% annualized (3.5%), durable goods +0.2% (+0.7%), claims 189,000; CME 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec; US 10-year 4.85% (−6 to −8 bp), 2-year 4.61%. The front-end gap is about 289 bp, the 10-year gap about 220 bp. Tokyo CPI 27 Nov: consensus core 2.9%, headline 3.1%, core-core 2.7%.","Yen and oil: USD/JPY closed 25 Nov in New York at 159.17 (OANDA; −0.09 from 159.26), with a 159.41 high after 23:00 JST and a 158.66 low before 02:00 JST as US stocks fell 3% to 4% intraday, then back as they recovered (Kyodo 159.14 to 159.24 at 17:00 New York); EUR/JPY 174.66 (+0.02), EUR/USD 1.0974 (+0.0008), dollar index 103.71 (−0.10%). Tokyo 26 Nov: 159.12 at 8:00, a 159.24 high before 9:00, a 158.95 low in the 9:00 hour before Tamura spoke, 159.17 at noon with the Nikkei up 1.5%, EUR/JPY 174.37 to 174.76; no visible reaction to the speech and no MoF or Katayama comment dated 25 or 26 Nov was found; US markets are closed 26 Nov and close early 27 Nov; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov. Oil: WTI $83.68 (+1.99%) and Brent $94.61 (+3.51%) after a Houthi strike on Ras Tanura; gold $3,534.70 (−0.71%) after trading under $3,500 for the first time since August 2025.","Markets: Tokyo 25 Nov close: Nikkei 57,011.62 (−1,633.01, −2.78%), open 58,383.49, low 56,502.97 (−2,141 intraday), Prime turnover ¥7.50tn on 2,734m shares, 993 up against 537 down (63.9%), 23 of 33 sectors up (pharmaceuticals +5.48%, real estate +3.54%, food +3.53%) against nonferrous metals −8.69%, electrical equipment −4.48%, glass −3.79%; Advantest about −762 points and Tokyo Electron −567 (together about 1,328 of the 1,633), SoftBank Group −379 points (−14.8%, year-to-date low), Ibiden −115, Fujikura 2,062.5 (−14.7%), Murata 4,633 (−14.2%); Fast Retailing 86,170 (+2,130, +172 points), Chugai 7,501 (+426), Ono Pharmaceutical +10.7% on an Amgen patent settlement; Nikkei VI 33.39 (+1.88, high 43.23); December futures 57,040 (−1,940), night session 57,370; TOPIX 3,746.20 (−18.68, −0.50%), Growth 250 640.60 (−2.2%). Wall Street 25 Nov: S&P 500 7,182.78 (−0.25%) after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq 23,585.80 (−0.62%) after −4.6%, Dow 50,182.95 (+0.33%) after −1,100 points, Russell 2000 2,704.06 (+0.71%), VIX 28.29 after topping 30; Nvidia $149.30 (−3.91%, low $141.17), AMD $406.03 (−1.75%), Micron −4.53%, Broadcom $300.86 (−0.07%), Anthropic $82.79 (+1.07%, under the $83 IPO), HP +11.49%; SOXX −0.50%, SMH −1.01% (the SOX close was not found). Tokyo 26 Nov morning: Nikkei 57,886.95 (+875.33, +1.54%) after 57,429.54 at the open and a rise of more than 1,200 to the 58,200s, 57,708.53 (+696.91) at 13:00; TOPIX 3,772.98 (+26.78, +0.71%); Prime 863 up against 629 down at 13:00, 20 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, oil, electrical equipment, glass) against air transport, precision and food; Advantest 25,260 (+1,515, +6.4%, +327 points), SoftBank Group 3,000 (+303.5, +11.3%, +246), Tokyo Electron 8,029 (+204, +103), Kioxia 7,732 (+837, +12.1%), TDK 2,231 (+4.2%); Fast Retailing 85,120 (−1,050, −85 points), Daikin −1.9%; futures 57,960 (+920) at noon; morning turnover ¥3.59tn. The first stabilisation test (60,030, 3,895, VI under 35) was 5.0% and 3.8% away at the 25 Nov close and 3.6% and 3.1% away at the 26 Nov morning levels."],"translation":[{"body":"The 25 Nov close (Nikkei 57,011.62, −2.78%; TOPIX 3,746.20, −0.50%) was the second session in which the index and the market diverged: Advantest and Tokyo Electron were about 1,328 of the 1,633 points, SoftBank Group made a year-to-date low, and 993 Prime stocks rose against 537 with 23 of 33 sectors up and pharmaceuticals +5.48%. The 26 Nov morning (57,886.95, +1.54%) was the same names in reverse after Wall Street recovered most of a 3% to 4.6% intraday fall. Under the framework the Nikkei 225 is now a levered expression of the SOX and Nvidia ($149.30, −3.91%) over a broad market that is being bought on the dip; the drawdown is 21.7% on the close with the liquidation low (56,436.65) untested, and the posture stays Risk-off until 60,030 and 3,895 are retaken with the Nikkei VI (33.39) under 35. The one new risk is the Section 301 proposal: 15% in total and autos exempt matches the deal, the 20% alternative does not.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"JGBs bull-flattened to 20 years on 25 Nov (2-year 1.720%, 5-year 2.030%, 10-year 2.645%, 20-year 3.580%, futures 128.24, the highest since 6 Nov) while the 30-year rose to 3.950% and the 40-year to 4.180% after the ¥300bn 40-year reopening cleared at 4.09%, above consensus, on 2.63 cover. The read under the framework: the haven bid is in the 2- to 20-year sector and is also pricing part of a BoJ hold (2-year under 1.75%, December 76.7% per MogWatch), the super-long end is supply and term premium, and the yen at 159.17 with the Nikkei down 2.78% showed a haven bid only when US stocks were down 3% to 4% (158.66 low), so the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on. Tamura's \"bring the rate to neutral soon\" and \"accelerate the pace\" on 26 Nov is the Bank pushing the other way; the 2-year close on 26 Nov, the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) and Ueda on 30 Nov decide which side gives. USD/JPY sits 83 sen from 160 with the front-end gap about 289 bp and no MoF comment since 19 Nov.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The BoJ is tightening into an equity drawdown of 21.7% from the record, with inflation at target (core CPI 2.2%), services PPI at a 36-year high (+4.2%), real wages positive (+2.2%) and the flash composite PMI at a six-month low (51.5); Tamura wants the rate at neutral soon and a faster pace if prices overshoot, and the market has trimmed December to about 77%. The Fed is pricing 97.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec after core PCE 3.3% and Q3 GDP 4.3%, so a BoJ hike to 1.50% leaves the gap unchanged; the pace difference, not the December move, is what matters for the yen. The USTR Section 301 proposal (Japan 15% in total, autos and semiconductors exempt, comments to 5 Jan, hearing 25 Jan) is an export and political watch item rather than a liquidity event. The fiscal side is the pressure point: a 40-year auction above consensus on weak cover, the MoF primary-dealer meeting on 26 Nov and investor meeting on 27 Nov on 2027 issuance, and the food consumption-tax cut (about ¥5tn a year, unfunded) in the Diet to 12 Dec. The balance sheet (¥626.2tn) is unchanged; the taper continues toward ¥2tn a month of JGB purchases from April 2027.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
